Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe for **adding an icon at runtime** when the style’s sprite does not already contain it.  
It works with any recent version of **MapLibre GL JS** (and, because the API is identical, with Mapbox GL JS as well).

---

## 1. Why the “missing sprite” error happens

When a layer’s `layout["icon-image"]` (or `layout["symbol-placement"]` etc.) refers to a name that is **not** present in the style’s sprite, MapLibre throws:

```
Error: Image "my‑icon" not found in style sprite.
```

The sprite is a static collection of PNGs (or a JSON‑packed sprite sheet) that is loaded once when the style is applied.  
If you need an icon that wasn’t baked into that sprite, you have to **inject the image into the style’s image registry yourself**.

---

## 2. The two‑step runtime workflow

1. **Load the image data** (from a URL, a data‑URI, a `<canvas>`, an `ImageBitmap`, etc.).  
2. **Register it with the map** via `map.addImage(imageId, image, options)`.

After the image is registered you can reference it in any layer just like a sprite‑based icon:

```js
layout: {
  "icon-image": "my‑dynamic‑icon",   // <-- the same id you used in addImage()
  "icon-size": 1.2,
  // …other layout properties
}
```

If you try to add the same id twice you’ll get an error, so either:

* **Check first** with `map.hasImage(id)` and skip the add, **or**
* **Replace** an existing image with `map.updateImage(id, newImage)` (available in MapLibre ≥ 2.4).

---

## 3. Loading the image – the most common patterns

### 3.1. From a remote PNG/JPG (the simplest case)

```js
function addRemoteIcon(map, id, url, options = {}) {
  // 1️⃣ Wait until the style is ready (otherwise addImage will throw)
  if (!map.isStyleLoaded()) {
    map.once('styledata', () => addRemoteIcon(map, id, url, options));
    return;
  }

  // 2️⃣ Load the image (MapLibre provides a helper that returns an HTMLImageElement)
  map.loadImage(url, (err, image) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error(`Failed to load ${url}:`, err);
      return;
    }

    // 3️⃣ Register it – `options` can contain {sdf, pixelRatio, stretchX, stretchY, content}
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) {
      map.addImage(id, image, options);
      console.log(`✅ Added icon "${id}"`);
    } else {
      // optional: replace the existing image (useful for live‑updates)
      map.updateImage(id, image);
    }
  });
}
```

**Usage**

```js
addRemoteIcon(map, 'my-dynamic-icon', 'https://example.com/icons/rocket.png', {
  // Example: make it a signed‑distance‑field so you can recolor it with `icon-color`
  sdf: true,
  // If you need retina support, set pixelRatio to window.devicePixelRatio
  pixelRatio: window.devicePixelRatio
});
```

### 3.2. From a **data‑URI** (inline SVG, base64 PNG, etc.)

```js
function addDataUriIcon(map, id, dataUri, options = {}) {
  const img = new Image();
  img.onload = () => {
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, img, options);
  };
  img.onerror = (e) => console.error('Data‑URI image failed to load', e);
  img.src = dataUri;   // triggers the load
}
```

### 3.3. From a **Canvas** (perfect for programmatic icons)

```js
function addCanvasIcon(map, id, drawFn, options = {}) {
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = 64;   // choose a size that matches your design
  canvas.height = 64;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  drawFn(ctx, canvas); // <-- you draw whatever you want here

  // Convert to ImageBitmap (faster for the GPU) – optional but recommended
  if ('createImageBitmap' in window) {
    createImageBitmap(canvas).then((bitmap) => {
      if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, bitmap, options);
    });
  } else {
    // Fallback: use the canvas element directly
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, canvas, options);
  }
}
```

**Example draw function**

```js
function drawStar(ctx, canvas) {
  const r = canvas.width / 2;
  ctx.translate(r, r);
  ctx.beginPath();
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    ctx.lineTo(0, -r);
    ctx.translate(0, -r);
    ctx.rotate((Math.PI * 2) / 10);
    ctx.lineTo(0, r);
    ctx.translate(0, r);
    ctx.rotate(-(Math.PI * 6) / 10);
  }
  ctx.closePath();
  ctx.fillStyle = '#ff6600';
  ctx.fill();
}
```

```js
addCanvasIcon(map, 'star-icon', drawStar, { sdf: false });
```

### 3.4. From an **ImageBitmap** you already have (e.g., fetched via `fetch` + `createImageBitmap`)

```js
async function addBitmapIcon(map, id, bitmap, options = {}) {
  if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, bitmap, options);
}

// Example usage:
fetch('https://example.com/icons/plane.png')
  .then(r => r.blob())
  .then(blob => createImageBitmap(blob))
  .then(bitmap => addBitmapIcon(map, 'plane-icon', bitmap, { sdf: false }));
```

---

## 4. Hooking the icon into a layer

### 4.1. Simple point‑layer that uses the dynamic icon

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-points',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: {
      type: 'FeatureCollection',
      features: [
        {
          type: 'Feature',
          geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.45, 37.78] },
          properties: { name: 'Dynamic Rocket' }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  layout: {
    // The id you passed to addImage()
    'icon-image': 'my-dynamic-icon',
    // Optional: allow overlap so the icon never gets hidden
    'icon-allow-overlap': true,
    // Optional: scale the icon
    'icon-size': 1.0
  },
  paint: {
    // If you added the icon as an SDF you can recolor it here
    'icon-color': '#ff6600'
  }
});
```

### 4.2. Data‑driven icons (different icons per feature)

If you have a property that tells you which icon to use, you can reference **any** image that has been added at runtime:

```js
// Assume each feature has a property `iconId` that matches an image you added earlier
map.addLayer({
  id: 'dynamic-icons',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-geojson-source',
  layout: {
    'icon-image': ['get', 'iconId'],   // data‑driven
    'icon-allow-overlap': true,
    'icon-size': 0.8
  }
});
```

**Tip:** Before you set the `icon-image` expression, make sure *all* possible values have been added with `addImage`. If a feature references a missing id, the layer will simply not draw that point (no error is thrown).

---

## 5. Handling “missing‑icon” gracefully

You can intercept the error that MapLibre throws when a layer tries to use an unknown image and fall back to a default icon:

```js
map.on('error', (e) => {
  // Only react to the specific “image not found” error
  if (e.error && e.error.message?.includes('Image') && e.error.message?.includes('not found')) {
    const missingId = e.error.message.match(/Image "([^"]+)"/)[1];
    console.warn(`Icon "${missingId}" missing – swapping to fallback`);

    // If we already have a fallback, just replace the reference in the layer
    const fallbackId = 'fallback-icon';
    if (!map.hasImage(fallbackId)) {
      // Load the fallback once (you could also have added it at init time)
      addRemoteIcon(map, fallbackId, '/assets/fallback.png');
    }

    // Find every layer that uses the missing id and replace it
    map.getStyle().layers.forEach((layer) => {
      const layout = map.getLayoutProperty(layer.id, 'icon-image');
      if (layout === missingId) {
        map.setLayoutProperty(layer.id, 'icon-image', fallbackId);
      }
    });
  }
});
```

This pattern is handy when you are **driving the icon name from a remote dataset** that may contain values you haven’t pre‑loaded.

---

## 6. Performance & best‑practice checklist

| ✅ What to do | ❌ What to avoid |
|--------------|-----------------|
| **Batch add images** – if you know you’ll need 20 icons, load them all up front (e.g., in `map.on('styledata')`). | Adding a new image on every `move`/`zoom` event – that forces the GPU to re‑upload textures constantly. |
| **Reuse the same `imageId`** for identical icons. | Adding duplicate IDs (`map.addImage('foo

