Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑copy** example that shows how to pull every piece of the camera state you asked for from a MapLibre GL JS map:

* **center** – longitude / latitude (`LngLat` object)  
* **zoom** – numeric zoom level  
* **bearing** – rotation around the vertical axis (degrees)  
* **pitch** – tilt of the camera (degrees)  
* **projection matrix** – the 4 × 4 matrix that MapLibre uses to turn world‑space coordinates into clip‑space (the same matrix you get in a custom layer’s `render` callback)

---

## 1️⃣  Minimal HTML page

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Get Camera State</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    #info { position:absolute; top:10px; left:10px; background:#fff;
            padding:8px; font-family:monospace; font-size:13px;
            max-height:90%; overflow:auto; border-radius:4px; }
  </style>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div id="info"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Create the map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style:    'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center:   [0, 0],
    zoom:     2,
    pitch:    45,
    bearing:  30,
    antialias: true               // needed if you later use a custom WebGL layer
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Helper – read the full camera state
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  /**
   * Returns an object with the current camera state.
   *
   * @param {maplibregl.Map} map  The MapLibre map instance
   * @returns {{
   *   center: [number, number],
   *   zoom: number,
   *   bearing: number,
   *   pitch: number,
   *   projectionMatrix: number[]
   * }}
   */
  function getCameraState(map) {
    // Public getters (documented API)
    const center  = map.getCenter();          // LngLat object
    const zoom    = map.getZoom();
    const bearing = map.getBearing();         // degrees
    const pitch   = map.getPitch();           // degrees

    // The projection matrix lives on the internal `transform` object.
    // It is a Float32Array(16) in column‑major order (the same layout WebGL expects).
    // ⚠️  This is **not** part of the public API, but it is stable across
    //      MapLibre releases and is the only way to read the matrix directly.
    const projMatrix = map.transform.projMatrix;   // Float32Array

    // If you need a plain JS array (e.g. for console.log or JSON.stringify)
    const projMatrixArray = Array.from(projMatrix);

    return {
      center: [center.lng, center.lat],
      zoom,
      bearing,
      pitch,
      projectionMatrix: projMatrixArray
    };
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Show the state in the UI (updates on every move)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const infoDiv = document.getElementById('info');

  function updateInfo() {
    const state = getCameraState(map);
    infoDiv.innerHTML = `
      <strong>Camera state</strong><br>
      center   : ${state.center.map(v => v.toFixed(5)).join(', ')}<br>
      zoom     : ${state.zoom.toFixed(3)}<br>
      bearing  : ${state.bearing.toFixed(2)}°<br>
      pitch    : ${state.pitch.toFixed(2)}°<br>
      <strong>Projection matrix (first 4 rows)</strong><br>
      ${state.projectionMatrix.slice(0, 16).map(v => v.toExponential(3)).join('<br>')}
    `;
  }

  // Update once the map is ready
  map.once('load', updateInfo);

  // And on every interaction that changes the camera
  map.on('move', updateInfo);
  map.on('zoom', updateInfo);
  map.on('rotate', updateInfo);
  map.on('pitch', updateInfo);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What you see | How it works |
|------|--------------|--------------|
| **Create the map** | `new maplibregl.Map({...})` | Standard MapLibre initialization. |
| **Read the state** | `getCameraState(map)` | Uses the **public** getters (`getCenter()`, `getZoom()`, …) and the **internal** `map.transform.projMatrix`. |
| **Show it** | UI updates in the top‑left corner | Listens to `move`, `zoom`, `rotate`, and `pitch` events so the display stays in sync with the map. |

---

## 2️⃣  Accessing the matrix in a **custom WebGL layer**

If you are already writing a custom layer, MapLibre passes the combined **projection × view** matrix to you on every frame:

```js
const myCustomLayer = {
  id: 'my-layer',
  type: 'custom',
  renderingMode: '3d',          // or '2d' if you only need 2‑D
  onAdd: function (map, gl) {
    // initialise shaders, buffers, etc.
  },
  render: function (gl, matrix) {
    // `matrix` is a Float32Array(16) = projection * view
    // You can use it directly in your shader:
    //   gl.uniformMatrix4fv(u_matrixLocation, false, matrix);
  }
};

map.addLayer(myCustomLayer);
```

If you need the **projection‑only** matrix (without the view transform) you can still read it from `map.transform.projMatrix` as shown above, or you can extract it from the combined matrix:

```js
// In the render() callback:
const projMatrix = map.transform.projMatrix; // same object that MapLibre uses internally
```

---

## 3️⃣  Why the matrix lives on `map.transform`

* `map.transform` is the low‑level engine that handles all camera math (pan, zoom, pitch, bearing, globe vs. mercator, etc.).
* `projMatrix` is a 4 × 4 column‑major matrix (`Float32Array(16)`) that MapLibre builds each frame from:
  * the **projection** (perspective or orthographic, depending on the style’s `projection` property)
  * the **viewport size**
  * the **camera altitude** derived from the zoom level
* It is the exact matrix you would feed to a vertex shader if you want to render your own geometry in the same coordinate system as the map.

> **⚠️  Note:** `map.transform` is not part of the official public API. It is, however, stable across MapLibre releases and is the only way to read the raw matrix. If you ever upgrade to a future major version and the property disappears, you can fall back to the matrix that MapLibre hands to you in a custom layer’s `render` callback (the `matrix` argument).

---

## 4️⃣  Quick “one‑liner” for the most common case

If you just need the four *named* camera values (center, zoom, bearing, pitch) you can also use the **FreeCameraOptions** helper that MapLibre ships with:

```js
const opts = map.getFreeCameraOptions();   // {center, zoom, bearing, pitch, padding}
console.log('center  :', opts.center.toArray()); // [lng, lat]
console.log('zoom    :', opts.zoom);
console.log('bearing :', opts.bearing);
console.log('pitch   :', opts.pitch);
```

But **`getFreeCameraOptions()` does not expose the projection matrix**, so you still need the `map.transform.projMatrix` trick shown earlier.

---

## 5️⃣  TL;DR – The minimal code you need

```js
// Assuming `map` is your maplibregl.Map instance:
const cam = {
  center: map.getCenter().toArray(),   // [lng, lat]
  zoom:   map.getZoom(),
  bearing: map.getBearing(),
  pitch:   map.getPitch(),
  // 4×4 projection matrix (Float32Array) – internal but widely used
  projectionMatrix: map.transform.projMatrix
};

console.log(cam);
```

That `cam` object now contains everything you asked for, ready for any custom math you want to perform (e.g., converting geographic coordinates to clip‑space, intersecting rays with 3‑D objects, etc.). Happy coding! 🚀

