Let's craft answer.

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready example** that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single‑file container for vector tiles) in the browser.  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol that MapLibre GL JS understands.  
3. Add the PMTiles file as a **vector source**.  
4. Create a **line layer** that draws one of the source’s layers and style it (color, width, dash, etc.).

You can run the example as a single HTML file or split it into separate files if you prefer a build system (Webpack, Vite, etc.). All the libraries are loaded from public CDNs, so no local installation is required.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre GL + PMTiles – line layer demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- --------------------------------------------------- -->
<!-- 2️⃣  Load the required JS libraries from CDNs          -->
<!-- --------------------------------------------------- -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@2.7.0/dist/pmtiles.min.js"></script>

<script>
/* -------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Register the pmtiles:// protocol
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
const { PMTiles } = window.PMTiles;   // the library puts itself on window

// Replace this URL with the location of your own .pmtiles file.
// It can be a relative path, an absolute URL, or a data‑uri.
const PMTILES_URL = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/visgl/deck.gl-data/master/geojson/earthquakes/earthquakes.pmtiles";

/* Create a PMTiles object that knows how to fetch the file */
const pmtiles = new PMTiles(PMTILES_URL);

/* Tell MapLibre how to resolve a request that starts with "pmtiles://" */
maplibregl.addProtocol("pmtiles", (request) => {
  // request.url looks like "pmtiles://myfile.pmtiles/12/3456/7890.pbf"
  // The PMTiles library expects just the path after the protocol.
  const url = request.url.replace(/^pmtiles:\/\//, "");
  return pmtiles.get(url);
});

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Initialise the MapLibre map
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: "map",
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // A simple raster basemap (OpenStreetMap tiles) – you can replace it.
      "osm": {
        type: "raster",
        tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: "osm",
        type: "raster",
        source: "osm"
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],   // lon, lat
  zoom: 2
});

/* -------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  When the map is ready, add the PMTiles vector source
   ------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on("load", () => {
  // 5a️⃣  Add the vector source that points to the PMTiles file.
  map.addSource("my-pmtiles-source", {
    type: "vector",
    // The URL must start with the protocol we just registered.
    url: "pmtiles://" + PMTILES_URL
    // If you are using a relative path you could also write:
    // url: "pmtiles://./data/my-data.pmtiles"
  });

  // 5b️⃣  Find out which source‑layers exist inside the PMTiles.
  // This is optional but handy for debugging.  It prints the list
  // of layer names to the console.
  const source = map.getSource("my-pmtiles-source");
  source.on("data", (e) => {
    if (e.sourceDataType === "metadata") {
      console.log("Available source‑layers:", source.vectorLayers);
      // Example output: [{ id: "roads", ... }, { id: "water", ... }]
    }
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  Add a line layer that draws one of the source layers.
  // -------------------------------------------------------
  // Replace "roads" with the actual name of the layer you want to draw.
  // You can see the correct name in the console output from step 5b.
  const SOURCE_LAYER_NAME = "roads";   // <-- change this if needed

  map.addLayer({
    id: "my-line-layer",
    type: "line",
    source: "my-pmtiles-source",
    "source-layer": SOURCE_LAYER_NAME,   // <-- the vector layer inside the PMTiles
    layout: {
      // Optional: make lines join smoothly
      "line-join": "round",
      "line-cap": "round"
    },
    paint: {
      // ---- Basic styling -------------------------------------------------
      "line-color": "#ff6600",          // orange
      "line-width": [
        "interpolate",
        ["linear"],
        ["zoom"],
        5, 0.5,   // at zoom 5 → 0.5 px
        12, 2,   // at zoom 12 → 2 px
        18, 8    // at zoom 18 → 8 px
      ],
      "line-opacity": 0.9,

      // ---- Optional extra effects ----------------------------------------
      // Dashed line (you can comment this out if you want a solid line)
      "line-dasharray": [2, 4],

      // Glow effect (requires MapLibre GL JS ≥ 4.0)
      // "line-blur": 1,
      // "line-color": "rgba(255, 102, 0, 0.6)"
    }
  });
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 📚 What each part does

| Section | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| **HTML head** | Loads MapLibre GL CSS (so the map canvas gets the right size) and defines a full‑screen `<div id="map">`. |
| **CDN scripts** | `maplibre-gl` (the map engine) and `pmtiles` (the tiny library that knows how to read a `.pmtiles` file in the browser). |
| **Protocol registration** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', …)` tells MapLibre how to fetch a tile when the URL starts with `pmtiles://`. The `PMTiles` object does the heavy lifting (byte‑range requests, caching, etc.). |
| **Map creation** | A minimal style with an OSM raster basemap. You can replace the basemap with any other raster or vector style you like. |
| **Adding the vector source** | `type: "vector"` + `url: "pmtiles://<your‑file>"`. The source now points to the PMTiles container. |
| **Discovering source‑layers** | `source.vectorLayers` (available after the `metadata` event) lists the names of the layers stored inside the PMTiles file. Use the console output to pick the correct name for the next step. |
| **Line layer** | `type: "line"` draws the geometry. The `paint` object controls colour, width, opacity, dash pattern, etc. The width is interpolated with zoom so the line stays visible at all scales. |
| **Layout** | `line-join` and `line-cap` make the line look smooth at corners and ends. |

---

## 🛠️ How to adapt this to **your own** PMTiles file

1. **Host the `.pmtiles` file** where the browser can fetch it (same origin, a CDN, or a public URL).  
   - If you serve it from the same folder as the HTML, you can use a relative URL: `url: "pmtiles://./my-data.pmtiles"`.  
   - If you need authentication, you can add the appropriate `Authorization` header inside the protocol handler (see the `pmtiles` docs for `pmtiles.setHeaders`).  

2. **Find the correct `source-layer` name**:  
   - Open the page, open the browser console, and look for the line `Available source‑layers:`.  
   - Pick the `id` you want (e.g., `"roads"`, `"railways"`, `"contours"`).  

3. **Style the line** the way you like:  
   - `line-color`: any CSS colour string (`"#ff6600"`, `"rgb(0,120,255)"`, `"hsl(120,50%,50%)"`).  
   - `line-width`: can be a constant number or a zoom‑dependent expression (as shown).  
   - `line-dasharray`: an array of numbers `[dash, gap, dash, …]`.  
   - `line-opacity`, `line-blur`, `line-gradient`, etc., are also available.  

4. **Add more layers** (e.g., a fill layer for polygons, a symbol layer for labels) by repeating the `map.addLayer` call with a different `type` and `paint`/`layout` properties.

